Story: First cup
  As a tea drinker
  I want to start the kettle by turning the dial
  So that it works like the one I already have

  You want boiling water for a cup of black tea. The kettle starts the way an old stovetop
  knob does: turn the [[dial]] past the dashed 55 % mark and hold it there for a moment. The
  light turns red, steam rises and the [[display]] reads HEATING.

  The dial is only a switch. How hot the water gets is the preset on the display, which starts
  on WARM (70 °C), so press PRESET until it reads BOIL first (see [[Choosing a preset]]). The
  WATER line shows the temperature as it rises, and the kettle holds the water there until you
  turn it off.

  To turn it off, turn the dial back below the mark first, then press RESET. With the dial
  still up, RESET would only start it again.

  Scenario: Set it to boil, turn it up, then turn it off
    When the user presses preset
    Then the preset is HOT
    When the user presses preset
    Then the preset is BOIL
    When the dial is 80 %
    Then the kettle is heating
    When the dial is 0 %
    And the user presses reset
    Then the kettle is off

Story: Choosing a preset
  As someone who drinks oolong
  I want to pick how hot the water gets before I start
  So that the water suits the leaves

  The preset is the temperature the kettle heats to and then holds: WARM is 70 °C, HOT is
  85 °C and BOIL is 100 °C. Green tea is usually made around 70 °C, oolong around 85 °C and
  black tea with boiling water. The kettle is on WARM when you plug it in.

  PRESET steps through WARM, HOT and BOIL and back to WARM, and the PRESET line on the
  [[display]] shows the one you have. START starts the kettle without touching the dial, and
  with the dial down, RESET turns it off again once you have poured. PRESET, like the other
  buttons, does nothing while the child lock is on.

  Scenario: Step to HOT, start, and turn it off
    When the user presses preset
    Then the preset is HOT
    When the user presses start
    Then the kettle is heating
    When the user presses reset
    Then the kettle is off

Story: Keeping little hands off
  As a parent
  I want the child lock to keep the kettle off
  So that a curious toddler turning the dial doesn't start it

  LOCK turns the child lock on and off, and a CHILD LOCK sign shows while it is on. While it is
  on, the dial, START and PRESET do nothing, so a turned dial cannot start the kettle.

  The lock stops the dial from reaching the kettle; it does not move the dial. If the dial is
  still above the 55 % mark when you unlock, the kettle starts as soon as the lock comes off.
  So turn the dial down before you press LOCK again, as this tutorial does.

  Scenario: Turn the dial with the lock on, then unlock and boil
    When the user presses lock
    Then the child lock is on
    When the dial is 100 %
    Then the kettle is off
    When the dial is 0 %
    And the user presses lock
    Then the child lock is off
    When the dial is 80 %
    Then the kettle is heating

Story: When the kettle boils dry
  As a tea drinker
  I want to know when the kettle has boiled dry
  So that I refill it before I use it again

  If the water runs out while the kettle is heating, it stops by itself: the light goes out
  and the display reads DRY, with "Refill, then press reset". The kettle stays DRY until you
  reset it. Refill it, turn the dial below the 55 % mark, and press RESET; with the dial still
  up, it would start heating again straight away.

  Scenario: Boil dry, then refill and reset
    When the dial is 80 %
    Then the kettle is heating
    When the water runs out
    Then the kettle is dry
    When the dial is 0 %
    And the user presses reset
    Then the kettle is off
